                <h2>1. Don't Rush: Identify the Real Reason First</h2>
<p>Many people immediately submit an appeal after receiving the "Your account has been disabled" email, only to get no response or be repeatedly rejected due to incomplete materials.</p>
<p><strong>Recommended first steps:</strong></p>
<ul>
<li>Check the "Account Status" page in your Google Ads backend</li>
<li>Match the violation type code in the email (IP infringement, deceptive content, suspicious payment, etc.)</li>
<li>Review any follow-up emails from the Google Ads policy team</li>
</ul>
<blockquote>
<p><strong>Key Principle:</strong> Appeal materials must address the specific issue. Find the suspension reason first, then prepare corresponding evidence.</p>
</blockquote>
<hr />
<h2>2. Appeal Materials Checklist: Prepare by Violation Type</h2>
<h3>Universal Requirements (Mandatory for Any Violation)</h3>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Account basics: Ad account ID, company name, registered email</li>
<li>[ ] Problem description: Concise explanation with commitment to correction</li>
<li>[ ] Business license or company registration document (English version)</li>
<li>[ ] Website URL (if website-related violation)</li>
<li>[ ] Clear commitment: Describe corrective measures already taken</li>
</ul>
<h3>Specific Materials by Violation Type</h3>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Violation Type</th>
<th>Additional Requirements</th>
<th>Notes</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>IP Complaints</td>
<td>Trademark/patent authorization, supplier agreement</td>
<td>Provide authentic authorization chain</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Deceptive Content</td>
<td>Real product images, user review screenshots, compliance certifications</td>
<td>Prove content authenticity</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Suspicious Payment</td>
<td>Credit card statement, company bank statement, payment proof</td>
<td>Confirm account holder matches</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Policy Circumvention</td>
<td>Domain change records, technical whitepaper</td>
<td>Explain non-intentional violation</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<hr />
<h2>3. Appeal Letter: 3 Core Elements</h2>
<h3>Opening: State Your Request Directly</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>Dear Google Ads Policy Team,<br />
I am writing to appeal the suspension of my Google Ads account (ID: xxx). Upon reviewing your notice, I understand the issue relates to [violation type].</p>
</blockquote>
<h3>Body: Explanation + Supporting Materials</h3>
<ul>
<li>Explain objective reasons (avoid shifting blame)</li>
<li>List completed corrective actions</li>
<li>Commit to future compliance plan</li>
</ul>
<h3>Closing: Request for Review</h3>
<blockquote>
<p>I have attached the relevant supporting documents and made the necessary corrections. I kindly request a review of my account. Thank you for your time.</p>
</blockquote>
<p><strong>Warning:</strong> Keep your appeal letter to 200-300 words. Overly long explanations make it harder for reviewers to find key points.</p>
<hr />
<h2>4. Pre-Submission Checklist</h2>
<ul>
<li>[ ] Policy clause reference number from suspension email recorded</li>
<li>[ ] All materials in English or with English translations</li>
<li>[ ] Business license and official documents are clear and unaltered</li>
<li>[ ] Attachments named properly (format: CompanyName_MaterialType.pdf)</li>
<li>[ ] Only one appeal per violation reason—avoid duplicate submissions</li>
<li>[ ] Track submission date; expect 3-5 business days for response</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<h2>5. Top 3 Reasons Appeals Get Rejected</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Mismatched Materials:</strong> Generic materials submitted without specific proof for the violation</li>
<li><strong>Incomplete Corrections:</strong> Claimed corrections made, but website/ad content unchanged</li>
<li><strong>Inconsistent Information:</strong> Website/products mentioned in letter don't match actual account content</li>
</ol>
<hr />
<h2>6. Prevention Tips: Daily Habits Matter More Than Appeals</h2>
<ul>
<li>Check Google Ads policy updates monthly</li>
<li>Preview ad creatives with policy check tools before launching</li>
<li>Prepare document templates in advance for sensitive industries (finance, healthcare, gaming)</li>
<li>Set up account health monitoring with alerts for unusual activity or policy warnings</li>
</ul>
<hr />
<p><strong>Bottom Line:</strong> Successful appeals depend not on emotional appeals, but on matching materials, complete evidence chains, and demonstrated corrections. This checklist ensures your appeal materials won't be rejected at the first screening.</p>
